<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">The Canterbury Tales</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1387–<a href="/years/1400">1400</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canterbury">Canterbury</a> Tales is a collection of stories in Middle English verse and prose by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geoffrey-chaucer">Geoffrey Chaucer</a>, written in the late fourteenth century and left unfinished at his death in 1400. It presents a group of pilgrims travelling from Southwark to the shrine of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thomas-becket">Thomas Becket</a> at Canterbury, each telling tales in a storytelling contest. It was among the first books printed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-caxton">William Caxton</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/the-canterbury-tales.jpg" alt="The Canterbury Tales" width="320" height="248" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: DemonDays64 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Canterbury_Tales_characters_around_table_%E2%80%94_traced.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The Canterbury Tales is an anthology of twenty-four stories, mostly in verse, written in Middle English by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geoffrey-chaucer">Geoffrey Chaucer</a> between 1387 and 1400. They are presented as part of a fictional storytelling contest among pilgrims travelling from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> to the shrine of Saint Thomas Becket at Canterbury Cathedral. The General Prologue introduces some 30 pilgrims, and Chaucer's stated plan was four stories from each, two on the way to Canterbury and two on the way back, about 120 in all. Most scholars think the work was incomplete at the end of his life. Chaucer began it during the years when he was Controller of Customs and Justice of the Peace from 1386 and Clerk of the King's Works from 1389.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>Frame tales were an established genre, but Chaucer's collection differs from most in its intense variation. Most collections kept to a theme, usually a religious one, and even in the Decameron the storytellers stuck to the theme of the day. A pilgrimage brought together a diverse company, and the contest invites the reader to compare tales in many genres, from the Knight's Tale of chivalry to others in lower styles. Two tales, Sir Topas and The Tale of Melibee, are told by Chaucer himself, who travels with the pilgrims in his own story.</p><p>The tales reflect the controversies of the late 14th century: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-schism">Western Schism</a>, Lollardy under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-wycliffe">John Wycliffe</a>, the abuse of indulgences by pardoners, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peasants-revolt">Peasants' Revolt</a> of 1381. They are among the first English literary works to mention paper. Several characters are religious figures, so religion is a major theme, and the Knight's Tale examines chivalry, which was in decline in Chaucer's day, though scholars dispute whether it was meant to show its flaws.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>No <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a> exists in Chaucer's own hand. There are 84 manuscripts and four incunabula editions, more than for any other vernacular English text except the Prick of Conscience. They vary in minor and major ways, and there is no agreement on a complete version or on Chaucer's intended order. Most modern editions divide the tales into ten Fragments, following the Ellesmere order, while Victorian editors used the nine Groups of F. J. Furnivall.</p><p>In the 15th century John Lydgate and Thomas Occleve praised Chaucer as the best English poet, and the Knight's Tale was the most respected. The incompleteness led medieval authors to add tales, such as the Tale of Beryn, to some <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a>. The work may have helped popularise the English vernacular in literature, as against French or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a>. Shakespeare and John Fletcher based The Two Noble Kinsmen on the Knight's Tale, first performed in 1613 or 1614.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “The Canterbury Tales” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The English language, transformed after 1066 by French vocabulary, became a major literary language with Geoffrey Chaucer’s <b>Canterbury Tales</b> in the late fourteenth century, and William Caxton printed the first books in England in the 1470s.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-england">Kingdom of England · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>In the 12th century Petrus Alphonsi put a version of The Tale of Attaf into Latin in his Disciplina Clericalis; motifs with parallels in the Nights appear in Chaucer's <b>The Canterbury Tales</b> and Boccaccio's Decameron, and a Romanian translation existed by the 17th century.</blockquote><a href="/work/one-thousand-and-one-nights">One Thousand and One Nights · Reception</a></li></ul></section>
